#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133697 :question/id #uuid "cb99a3e1-9cd1-5335-b20c-01ad598d14bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8929" :question/problem "![](images/71/71c58bf1b0fe4259ac50633a457f6a76639c0a0c188b66cce5e0fc3c7e5b0329.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n![](images/86/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$ everywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133702 :question/id #uuid "d6e8fac3-5dfc-5487-bd81-dc33aeae5f1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9097" :question/problem "![](images/83/83bd636f3a294c641e171c5a8281aaa7068e1931a06229064314e8f96efd0c3a.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} (x^2-3y)\\, \\mathrm{d}x + ( y^2+x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= x^2-3y$ and $Q(x,y)= y^2+x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -3, \\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\underset{\\partial  D}{∮} ( {x}^{2} - 3 y )  \\mathrm{d} x + ( {y}^{2} + x )  \\mathrm{d} y &  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } ( \\frac{\\partial  Q}{\\partial  x} - \\frac{\\partial  P}{\\partial  y} )  \\text{d} A \\\\  &  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } ( 1 - ( - 3 ) )  \\text{d} A \\\\  &  = \\underset{D}{\\iint } 4  \\text{d} A \\\\  &  = 4 \\underset{D}{\\iint }  \\text{d} A \\\\  &  = 4 \\cdot  \\text{Area} ( D ) \\\\  &  = 4 \\cdot  ( \\pi  \\cdot  {4}^{2} - \\pi  \\cdot  {2}^{2} ) \\\\  &  = 4 \\cdot  12 \\pi  \\\\  &  = 48 \\pi  .\\end{array}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133705 :question/id #uuid "3cbc5b4a-dde4-59b0-bc34-08c4d8776e0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9489" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3y-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{j}+(x-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j} + R\\,\\mathbf k$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}+\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x-y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((3y-2z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x-2z)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(2x-y)+6(3y-2z)-4(x-2z) \\\\  & =4x+14y-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf { F}$ at the point $(1,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(1,2,1) & =4(1)+14(2)-4(1) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133711 :question/id #uuid "a817c3c6-97bb-55e4-9a1e-04ed99d3eaab" :question/math-academy-id "e-9553" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ which of the following could be the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\left\\langle -y, \\: y\\right\\rangle$\n\nII. $\\left\\langle -2x, \\: 2x\\right\\rangle$\n\nIII. $\\left\\langle 5y\\sin x, \\: x-5\\cos x \\right\\rangle$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle P(x,y), \\: Q(x,y) \\right\\rangle,$ where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case, \n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -1$\nand\n$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does not give the required area. In this case, \n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0$\nand\n$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 5\\sin x$\nand\n$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 1+5\\sin x,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133712 :question/id #uuid "74c9ecfb-5005-53ff-b7a9-db798488587b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9554" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2 \\cos t \\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D) \n= \\iint\\limits_D  \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} -  \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right) \\mathrm{d}A\n= \\int_0^{2\\pi} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A}}}\\: \\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*\n\n![](images/a5/a5eeb5db90f9ee0d86a49e5bbf02629c7886fa019db7cff3c4d0e1eb43e8fd75.png)" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n- \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = \\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'(t) = \\left\\langle  2\\cos t ,\\:  -2 \\sin t \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $4\\cos^2 t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133713 :question/id #uuid "aa4400ff-09b0-596c-9857-c13881b72f73" :question/math-academy-id "e-9555" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C=C_1 \\cup C_2,$ where \n\n- $C_1$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}_1(t) =-2 t^4 \\: \\mathbf{i} + t^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in \\left[-1,1\\right],$ and\n\n- $C_2$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}_2(t) = -2\\: \\mathbf{i} -t \\: \\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in \\left[-1,1\\right].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region.\n\n![](images/14/147e8fdd97fc938ae5482b70d7215483956a860ee3ce69ae5bbebd111dcc343b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of $\\mathbf r_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf r_2(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-8{t}^{3}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot (-8{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{6}\\,\\text{d}t+0 \\\\  & =8[\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133714 :question/id #uuid "39577555-e9cb-563a-8f5e-8b9ea4b57364" :question/math-academy-id "e-9556" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^{x+y} \\, \\mathbf{i} + 3xy \\, \\mathbf{j}.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}) \\\\  & =3y-{e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-1,1)} & =3(1)-{e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =3-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133715 :question/id #uuid "18eef985-0fd4-5b08-8072-684ded227ab4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9557" :question/problem "![](images/16/16026d4ac7deeb30c1abb3a066df23a06a544116463b364b0a1e9e0bac0608c8.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 10, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -8, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 3,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ and $C_3$ are oriented clockwise, and $C_2$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves $C_2$ and $C_3$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ and $C_3$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/11/11445c083440ee2fde1864d599a8bbd68146d522c61de11b3e90dc231e6eddf1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve $C_1$ and the inner curve $C_2$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-10-(-8)+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133716 :question/id #uuid "defe326f-f578-568c-a9b3-3d01f67da058" :question/math-academy-id "e-9558" :question/problem "![](images/64/64ba92b021de2ba2f4fc98f1ce76fa0d16b3c8b7ed9ae45237cb8772b111c8b8.png)\n\n  \n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} (2x^3y^2-x^5) \\,\\mathrm{d}x + (3x^4y+y^4) \\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x^3y^2-x^5$ and $Q(x,y)= 3x^4y+y^4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 4x^3y, \\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 12x^3y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{5})\\,dx+(3{x}^{4}y+{y}^{4})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{3}y-4{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as $\\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 1, -1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 0, 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-16)\\cdot (4-1)-(0-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =(-15)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e37611b-b4ca-5379-847c-3a7765d404fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439393} {:answer/id #uuid "765faeb0-ed48-53f3-b1eb-c5a6f7e2135b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e37611b-b4ca-5379-847c-3a7765d404fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439393} :answer-field/id #uuid "b65cb51f-ba83-56b7-9dc2-4a765d918943" :answer-field/key "$\\text{div}\\,F=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "394b0c92-413b-5182-bf86-908b31bea2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439395} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a2bf22-912d-55ed-9446-5abda1fe8202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394b0c92-413b-5182-bf86-908b31bea2d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439395} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7014164-d3a8-54e0-b2b2-69b7a546b3ee" :answer-field/key "$\\text{div}\\,F>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d66ca4c-dc58-584c-976f-6c65dcb0374b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439397} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1a3581-91b7-51be-a39a-394d06c35627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b1a3581-91b7-51be-a39a-394d06c35627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439398} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c3322fa-b621-5555-b3ed-1f86ce1470ea" :answer-field/key "$\\text{div}\\,F<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440056} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c747585-3d2c-5bc7-bfaa-a6485611b22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439399} {:answer/id #uuid "36fe6723-4807-59df-ae7e-c3f3add34f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c747585-3d2c-5bc7-bfaa-a6485611b22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439399} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a24dae-9028-5f6f-bfb1-b8f7462e6a64" :answer-field/key "$F$ is solenoidal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d82a128a-970f-55e8-b4ad-4c3c16a14935" :question/math-academy-id "q-105075" :question/problem "![](images/34/3411e11060b06a11c82ad677f50731faa31c6bc2e83ccb0d0d10931cf3d76592.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like a source at $P.$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like a sink at $P.$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{div}\\,F<0$ is true, whereas statements $\\text{div}\\,F=0$ and $\\text{div}\\,F>0$ are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors decrease as we move in the $y$-direction. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{div}\\,F<0$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64adfa84-016b-5b75-bfaa-1a8a86ec6cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437398} {:answer/id #uuid "74db0023-87ca-59a4-a5bd-f3ec8e8116b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74db0023-87ca-59a4-a5bd-f3ec8e8116b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437399} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d82e25-5382-533b-84b7-01e9f7a1d6d9" :answer-field/key "$\\text{curl}\\,F=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438728} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8567ee77-d4f1-5260-9f23-476c33065db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437400} {:answer/id #uuid "a70669e9-818c-5056-9e48-6b53420d42e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8567ee77-d4f1-5260-9f23-476c33065db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437400} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ff09e7-c874-5a69-a376-7cc151c1b301" :answer-field/key "$\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438729} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdbd1a0c-db83-58a6-82a4-5124aff61305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437402} {:answer/id #uuid "66a86276-9dec-5106-ae18-146b9a8a0341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66a86276-9dec-5106-ae18-146b9a8a0341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437403} :answer-field/id #uuid "76eb44c2-5da3-5456-a3ae-c27f914fb121" :answer-field/key "$F$ is irrotational" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438730} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c36d028-9a19-523f-8a50-c043c1583d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437404} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c4fd28-7364-51cd-9702-c77f2ad2fba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c36d028-9a19-523f-8a50-c043c1583d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437404} :answer-field/id #uuid "455d8cf9-d20d-599d-bbf0-180199060744" :answer-field/key "$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a72f589-a1bb-57d0-9bf2-23704868e98f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105092" :question/problem "![](images/38/38a7ae83a66a2c1ff6a012b9405c6621b0c0a162018065549e75a1d08bc04d44.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ is true, whereas statement $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$ is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements “$F$ is irrotational” is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement “$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction” is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$; “$F$ is irrotational” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba17fd4c-8a29-5e53-98c2-ac71e936d451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439401} {:answer/id #uuid "829d51be-bbb4-5302-8892-638706b87aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba17fd4c-8a29-5e53-98c2-ac71e936d451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439401} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0a7c437-986c-533d-b681-b536d1087ea4" :answer-field/key "$curlF = 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7f91ce5-8027-5662-bf7f-89461f30876f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439403} {:answer/id #uuid "74982af7-ea24-566e-a318-31d4afed4a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74982af7-ea24-566e-a318-31d4afed4a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439404} :answer-field/id #uuid "95428b1e-403f-5e89-9282-91389dc3cc7d" :answer-field/key "$curlF \\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4cd6a1-3348-5907-a8f5-a077070f5905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439405} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc39078-e01a-5ae3-8435-7c5e5de22beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cc39078-e01a-5ae3-8435-7c5e5de22beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439406} :answer-field/id #uuid "20819904-04f5-57af-aaf8-c983f5553a47" :answer-field/key "$curlF$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b28c8347-5141-5361-802d-14904582c104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439407} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e427c3-d890-5a06-a66c-a11cc7473766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b28c8347-5141-5361-802d-14904582c104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439407} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c295151-fc81-5e26-83a2-0c544bc56dcc" :answer-field/key "$F$ is irrotational" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440061}] :question/id #uuid "4556fe88-939e-5892-9221-116e661c0c0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105103" :question/problem "![](images/04/04c6eb44298b639520994f01ef505ff40eba58307a1a40e7a48dfb0ebaae5d7e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z)$, shown above. Given that $F(x, y, z)$ is independent of $z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The vector field rotates clockwise with increasing magnitude away from the origin. Its curl is nonzero and points in the negative z-direction; it is not irrotational."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c79d74f-4409-57e4-b361-e941cc41aa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407618} {:answer/id #uuid "941fb884-9dbd-5e6e-bdb3-9c13326a2f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407620} {:answer/id #uuid "d9966256-2b26-5637-969d-ec652f55ff58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407624} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0916bb-4bf2-5bfb-8625-f74c2a28d4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xy}\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407626} {:answer/id #uuid "eecb625e-fb38-58c5-8c7d-425f0374e2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j+3y{z}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c79d74f-4409-57e4-b361-e941cc41aa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407618} :answer-field/id #uuid "fafbd1ee-fb02-5785-b3f4-4426b308c48e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db7680f4-57f3-5a1a-a81a-52c92b75f829" :question/math-academy-id "q-105555" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,i-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y{z}^{3}\\,k.$ \nFind $∇\\cdot F.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot F=\\text{div}\\,F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8d1e28e-5112-59c3-b028-1c974391ce13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130553} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0afd35-d0b9-5121-8868-93f155cf991e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130554} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0d2587-d570-5521-a8be-6d6e03efbaa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130555} {:answer/id #uuid "53a7d265-98f2-5172-b447-4723f569dfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130556} {:answer/id #uuid "773479c1-d247-55ed-8fde-2e38ee1a00fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130557}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2563952c-5ef7-53f3-b596-8bb6bcf0a4ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130552}] :question/id #uuid "68ce4c5f-1400-5d63-8353-a17b8fdc5ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-105561" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F = (e^{x}\\cos x)i + xe^{xy}j$.\nEvaluate $∇ \\cdot F$ at the point $(0, 1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3f7214e-b431-56d7-bfb7-264d923304a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439409} {:answer/id #uuid "28aa7527-fdde-5107-909c-8eac04e3521c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28aa7527-fdde-5107-909c-8eac04e3521c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439410} :answer-field/id #uuid "8de723b7-940f-5a5a-8f71-5147966d54e1" :answer-field/key "$\\text{div}\\,F=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a3396c-f648-59d7-86ad-e5a8c9e73b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439411} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6b0ab8-5bb8-53b1-a594-3981e92e91d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41a3396c-f648-59d7-86ad-e5a8c9e73b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439411} :answer-field/id #uuid "475fb5dd-c494-5648-876b-68b1e41539f1" :answer-field/key "$\\text{div}\\,F>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f50e153b-42df-5358-9d5f-1feae4851881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439413} {:answer/id #uuid "92c437ec-54a4-5c1c-9163-4b0d41cb2832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f50e153b-42df-5358-9d5f-1feae4851881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439413} :answer-field/id #uuid "36c6f469-c87b-5afd-80e2-4ce904844a8c" :answer-field/key "$\\text{div}\\,F<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440064} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6abbcd53-7265-58cb-bea9-68b80e7a66ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439415} {:answer/id #uuid "343af783-682f-5a27-8c10-4cc12c878c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "343af783-682f-5a27-8c10-4cc12c878c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439416} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca9d0ac4-aaa2-5c2b-8f03-ae41d78b45eb" :answer-field/key "$F$ is solenoidal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0a7d3d7-12b8-5fcb-bcca-49538501dfd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105571" :question/problem "![](images/86/864ce19530f022e52b7c9513c0cebc64e898f6e71ecf1066ab6aad92d6df40bf.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{div}\\,F=0$ is true, whereas statements $\\text{div}\\,F>0$ and $\\text{div}\\,F<0$ are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is solenoidal” is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{div}\\,F=0$; “$F$ is solenoidal” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe357184-c804-53e5-9673-6bcefc79fd9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439453} {:answer/id #uuid "0918b264-4f40-5876-987b-5c8750a26cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe357184-c804-53e5-9673-6bcefc79fd9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439453} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3372797-44ff-5f24-b147-8ec1e085072b" :answer-field/key "$P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=-x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0842fe57-c4b3-512a-a06a-8b846341aac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439455} {:answer/id #uuid "586a0c72-4a76-59c2-8374-26e889f264c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0842fe57-c4b3-512a-a06a-8b846341aac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439455} :answer-field/id #uuid "d69bbf46-abac-5660-b279-f3c0fe70f969" :answer-field/key "$P(x,y)=\\frac{x}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85cdaefa-89de-57a1-b19f-0c380903a4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439457} {:answer/id #uuid "0d933456-9721-5edd-b8fc-3dc9d94ba641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d933456-9721-5edd-b8fc-3dc9d94ba641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439458} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac6e472f-13b9-5a6e-8237-3f44c55c1cc5" :answer-field/key "$P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d72f3f4-6ff1-53f9-94a6-72b405d3f343" :question/math-academy-id "q-125852" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair “$P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=-x$” does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair “$P(x,y)=\\frac{x}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{2}$” does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair “$P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$” gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134093 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2b8c17c-21b8-507b-8420-d670c4b65f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439459} {:answer/id #uuid "17e98f8a-da18-55f0-8939-1564456b888b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2b8c17c-21b8-507b-8420-d670c4b65f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439459} :answer-field/id #uuid "182f9891-f2da-5a34-881d-2dc0e9bd1216" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,{x}^{2}+x⟩$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440087} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aff01827-2164-503c-885f-729ce07f69b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439461} {:answer/id #uuid "41f3acaf-c391-5b28-bea3-53dc115237b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41f3acaf-c391-5b28-bea3-53dc115237b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439462} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6bbea9d-55dc-56d4-af3c-6bb5a3355e6e" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{4}+x,\\,\\frac{3x}{4}⟩$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c35b0a14-7ff3-5770-b606-c1b4ba2b788d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439463} {:answer/id #uuid "c0470289-cbe4-59c9-abaf-f84a85ae4c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0470289-cbe4-59c9-abaf-f84a85ae4c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439464} :answer-field/id #uuid "f690e279-0ebb-573a-bc24-60532b449e8c" :answer-field/key "$F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,0⟩$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0916e138-1ac3-5dc1-b5d3-86d819d37c90" :question/math-academy-id "q-125853" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,{x}^{2}+x⟩$ does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x+2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{4}+x,\\,\\frac{3x}{4}⟩$ gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{3}{4},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,0⟩$ gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{4}+x,\\,\\frac{3x}{4}⟩$; $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,0⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99d308c6-8a57-5de5-bcc7-bf40d867d0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130721} {:answer/id #uuid "adb3a2f4-608c-5138-8635-60f40d20bbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130722} {:answer/id #uuid "b425488f-374f-5fbf-a20f-6ed75b115a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130723} {:answer/id #uuid "bda8a425-c21e-5b88-81a7-318e0cea26f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130724} {:answer/id #uuid "669852c3-9028-56fe-aab7-f1bce32786a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130725}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8fcbecb7-a504-5e86-b075-909351068095" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130720}] :question/id #uuid "64f58b26-9afd-5961-840b-da865c629781" :question/math-academy-id "q-126427" :question/problem "Given that $F(x, y) = xyi + yj$, what is the scalar curl of $F$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "613b73ad-b4bd-580f-91d8-dabafde0761e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409595} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf92fb5-00f1-572a-8207-d72045cf4a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409599} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5d28d8-413b-530d-ad6e-efa5df8905f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409601} {:answer/id #uuid "ee35c795-c099-5c49-977b-052171747158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409603} {:answer/id #uuid "f7113576-7a9c-589a-960b-40c8d014ca07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a5d28d8-413b-530d-ad6e-efa5df8905f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409601} :answer-field/id #uuid "9898eac0-330a-51e3-bb68-1c7f9a46f935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "494f0061-8555-5747-be24-aea11c4501ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-126428" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(\\pi ,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(\\pi ,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(\\pi ,0)} & =-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0-\\pi \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074f2b27-d82e-5431-893f-5cb420b7db07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130739} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb000df-3f30-5d92-833d-b570ce4d2fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130740} {:answer/id #uuid "67a5cc30-ac62-56fb-b984-6bf8c55a68e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130741} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1f5867-3adf-5ebd-8085-208cd0303ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130742} {:answer/id #uuid "6da1aafe-3858-50f0-8369-80cd33a8831e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "074f2b27-d82e-5431-893f-5cb420b7db07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130739} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d6f2a4f-8215-5cfe-aa3b-409cfed8d343" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d511ff2-5d88-51e6-9da4-d8015a261af1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126515" :question/problem "![](images/32/320a45751b2e718c66bb721cfcd78a2d206f08a9998fbbffddb1581ee995cf34.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}x\\,dy-y\\,dx,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x\\,dy-y\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-(-1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({4}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (16-4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc41a40b-917a-57ad-93c5-f9db0205c2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130746} {:answer/id #uuid "34e14694-9434-5ad9-aa2d-2969b9ae1cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2ce2f4-0374-5148-947b-96f3d8b6b4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130748} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7d99a7-294b-5cf9-90af-ed4075071bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130749} {:answer/id #uuid "323a1bfd-c415-5a52-8f82-c95d150e0d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34e14694-9434-5ad9-aa2d-2969b9ae1cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} :answer-field/id #uuid "59065be7-12d4-5585-856f-70e63556d4de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa43bdf8-83a8-50c7-a713-6a675dec7cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126558" :question/problem "![](images/72/7264984a1c19c247c030feae04615bb0c46205ca70475db1240db964ad6007b2.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=10,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/0d/0de833fcc39b6295418c1558cf1187774db7b4ada1d697f2dcac8c35858563df.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =10-4-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134099 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f1a9de-bb0b-570a-b792-70641d11475f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431286} {:answer/id #uuid "4724eac8-60ba-5daf-b39a-a8285610e36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431305} {:answer/id #uuid "84acf7b5-dff2-5f83-9da4-34dd8d7b51ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431314} {:answer/id #uuid "9970035c-1db5-5547-b617-95eb8f16e1ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431318} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc187d5-7c8b-56e2-94b6-83f788afb083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18f1a9de-bb0b-570a-b792-70641d11475f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431286} :answer-field/id #uuid "b24f5ff7-bafc-59bf-b1fe-ef9b2d41540e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5478739e-f4d5-5eec-8651-b4b73e6b218c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126566" :question/problem "![](images/52/52815b087f869769950d37ab6b95edb75accf030e742ed16b4679b6a41d7d638.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot ({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8672bfbf-4d4d-5253-9f83-f75552aab637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130757} {:answer/id #uuid "e05949f9-9eb7-5677-b997-22c6339b8950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130758} {:answer/id #uuid "ffdc5cdf-d5f3-58a1-ae3b-d8584e3667be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130759} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf2c260-85c4-50d2-8cbc-b06eda27ff5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130760} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7aa802-5a2e-5905-9460-5989c7b7829e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130761}] :answer-field/id #uuid "49e8a026-4e1e-5ba9-b726-87255f49ad4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d09c91eb-6025-563c-9e1e-422c46c45a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369381} {:answer/id #uuid "f7db36f3-e726-57a8-990c-cb139c3421dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369382} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9cd067-f29c-5dd2-92a7-39171feb6a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369383} {:answer/id #uuid "5df13d18-ed06-5111-b470-86ff1f65bc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369384} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab6388d-f385-5685-97ba-bf84a736e56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d9cd067-f29c-5dd2-92a7-39171feb6a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369383} :answer-field/id #uuid "e35c2b8b-a5ba-5e72-9086-b121a2efaac4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54b4afaf-80a9-50b2-aaaa-863d5d133db6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126578" :question/problem "![](images/52/52815b087f869769950d37ab6b95edb75accf030e742ed16b4679b6a41d7d638.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=1$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi -\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134101 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aeb67126-f2b3-5385-ae58-f6aa47dd1135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369394} {:answer/id #uuid "6a1f51d1-849c-5c40-98ed-fc855b6f7397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369395} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbc2426-d8bc-5d9e-b636-6dd81e32cf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369396} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3e82f7-ed7f-5766-8b2b-b24890ff949c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369397} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba0ac0c-d2ca-5864-b0a8-1497f9993024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "169" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cbc2426-d8bc-5d9e-b636-6dd81e32cf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "138" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369396} :answer-field/id #uuid "83851aef-4707-5c37-b41d-06a411ef4f5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23de2113-f2a7-50b0-94a3-c0894b2b5ab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126645" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bda5a10df8a1ec081db97e7aa52755796f1d50be5447e3e64f55986116ada6e.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{y}^{2})-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le 4,-3\\le y\\le 3\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-(-4))\\cdot (9-(-9))-(2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 18-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =138.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8dac37b-0c3a-5985-b30b-b455cf17748a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130769} {:answer/id #uuid "0f82c5b5-9ec9-5493-9c5e-088d8f3b2063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130770} {:answer/id #uuid "226dc38b-e879-5d2e-a506-237fa0f69c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130771} {:answer/id #uuid "6b90ef5b-f492-5c0a-b77c-3339c17e5782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130772} {:answer/id #uuid "6c604190-84cc-5e3c-a8c7-3b023549d9b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "226dc38b-e879-5d2e-a506-237fa0f69c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130771} :answer-field/id #uuid "031169e4-3e81-5b19-b1f4-b5af34d5e90a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0e6048a-c528-5a8c-b47d-ee8959e52035" :question/math-academy-id "q-126654" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbe865077b446bdd97c290cef0924d3f17807a9e1039c9be45f4aa6e4b29cc3c.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=5,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fd/fd221ae68fc4983fb0a3f7a745154764e5c0081b2b9b66e7f8ad4a150a8b3bc5.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the inner curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are opposite to those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =1-5-(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffd3c9ec-3cf7-567b-a353-7585d49bb4b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} + z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130967} {:answer/id #uuid "011715b2-f35f-5761-81b5-48d58c2b0ac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130968} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6bded5-6334-5d42-977c-94cb680710d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130969} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2e7185-13ca-551c-b2b9-a1f06717c040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y + z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130970} {:answer/id #uuid "7c572ba2-1637-5089-bb75-49b67db7af89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130971}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7d720396-d9d8-5526-a6c9-d783c2ddfa07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130966}] :question/id #uuid "9360d1cf-9517-569c-8e77-dfd2eb8609da" :question/math-academy-id "q-162407" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x = 1$, $x = 3$, $y =-1$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F = xzi + y^{2}j + k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}A A A A Adzdydx.\n$$\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b4e90d-c970-54c3-ab06-a30ba09864e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\,z+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278607} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebe8543-ec3e-587e-9768-a831766e3eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279734} {:answer/id #uuid "aa98d390-38c9-5242-9163-03ea126a9877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280105} {:answer/id #uuid "acad9a07-0f5d-5457-86b1-82efec638ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280120} {:answer/id #uuid "ea617dc5-3c2b-587f-bfa4-9046fb77867d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa98d390-38c9-5242-9163-03ea126a9877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280105} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ee8cb1-a891-5ada-b9cf-ae248d766e50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "152cbc4e-4f16-538e-9423-127439b0fd3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162408" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c423938-da5c-5986-904d-ca90bf43b04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278710} {:answer/id #uuid "1603c0df-af5e-5636-b1a6-72475e40234a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278787} {:answer/id #uuid "1d02c2fc-86f7-5eda-b6b6-62da495ea6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278860} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1ed5c0-e3de-599b-b026-5cb574362292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{400\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279264} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc2db2a-7618-540a-a2a1-b6f665e7242c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{800\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d02c2fc-86f7-5eda-b6b6-62da495ea6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278860} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ce83a8-eea0-5bfa-a466-b577953f924a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "088370e5-5be7-5c78-9dd1-180055943e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-162410" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $F=x\\,i+xz\\,j+3z\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60179c77-308c-5a4b-9b41-da233c3efd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374181} {:answer/id #uuid "c11f9a42-43be-517a-94cc-42c6ba595526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374182} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d48b0f-7937-5ff0-8ff5-b0a889036dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374183} {:answer/id #uuid "ae328f95-387f-55bc-b6b1-78da1a0e6c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374184} {:answer/id #uuid "9829f33d-f493-59a1-b8be-a91cbaec38dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60179c77-308c-5a4b-9b41-da233c3efd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374181} :answer-field/id #uuid "a29f5580-8460-5ca2-bf68-68db1fc8ae7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de14125f-fde2-50fc-9770-aae06e6d9c30" :question/math-academy-id "q-162411" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,2y,\\,{x}^{2}+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0+2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfbacc2c-3295-5427-aa6d-abb9d46ca58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130991} {:answer/id #uuid "068f8b0d-155c-5288-ab99-7bef355ce630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130992} {:answer/id #uuid "217bf025-09a6-5388-b75e-9b4aa6c0931a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130993} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8b540c-c595-5796-b0a9-c06e4f0ff6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130994} {:answer/id #uuid "4935e6a8-5d04-5659-bd19-c0da643e24ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "068f8b0d-155c-5288-ab99-7bef355ce630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130992} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf5d99a-43d2-5f4d-9cab-2d45271876ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4d6f3d3-e8a0-54e3-96bf-06ac782d45a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162412" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=2xz\\,i+({x}^{2}+z)\\,j+2\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2) \\\\  & =2z+0+0 \\\\  & =2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(1-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
